<details class="details" id="details">
  <summary>Details and sources</summary>
  <div class="prose-block">
    <!-- 1. Explainer -->
    <h3>The research in plain English</h3>
    <p>
      A guide for non-specialists to the March 2026 report
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15953">“A Family of LLMs Liberated from Static Vocabularies”</a>.
    </p>

    <h4>What a tokenizer does</h4>
    <p>
      Before a language model reads text, a tool called a tokenizer cuts it into pieces called tokens, like a fixed set of rubber stamps
      made before training. Common English words have their own stamp. In
      <a href="https://aleph-alpha.com/en/blog/breaking-free-of-tokenizers/">Aleph Alpha’s example</a>, the German
      <em lang="de">Bundeskanzler</em> needs four pieces while <em>chancellor</em> needs one. The model sees only each stamp’s ID number,
      so “car” and “cars” can look unrelated.
    </p>

    <h4>What HAT does differently</h4>
    <p>
      HAT (Hierarchical Autoregressive Transformer) drops the stamp set. A small encoder reads the raw bytes, the units computers use to
      store letters, and condenses each word into one summary. The backbone, the large main part of the model, works one word at a time.
      A small decoder writes the reply byte by byte. Because HAT starts from letters, similar spellings get similar summaries.
    </p>

    <h4>What the report shows</h4>
    <p>
      The report covers models with up to 70 billion parameters, the adjustable numbers a model learns in training. A 7B model was
      trained from scratch, and Meta’s Llama 3.1 models (8B and 70B) were converted to HAT. Benchmarks are standard tests for comparing
      models.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tokenizer-free models were competitive with tokenizer-based ones, even when trained on one third of Llama 3.1’s data.</li>
      <li>German ARC Challenge (school science questions): 59.1% for the 7B model, 47.4% for Llama 3.1 8B.</li>
      <li>On German text, each backbone step covered 6.03 bytes on average, against 3.33 for Llama 3.1 8B. Fewer steps can mean less computing.</li>
      <li>The converted 8B chat model won 71.0% of English and 73.9% of German MT-Bench comparisons, scored by an AI judge, against Llama 3.1 8B Instruct.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Why it matters</h4>
    <p>
      Tokenizers are tuned mostly for English, so German and specialist terms, as in contracts or technical specifications, break into
      more pieces. Because HAT reads letters, the report says it copes better with spelling differences such as typos and adapts to new
      languages and fields with further training.
    </p>

    <h4>What it does not show yet</h4>
    <p>
      The models cover English and German only and were not tuned for code or maths. Under heavy load, current serving software produces
      less output per second than with a comparable tokenizer-based model. The 70B model is experimental and scores lower than Llama 3.3
      70B Instruct on some academic tests. The authors say the robustness benefits need more testing.
    </p>

    <h4>Who can use the models</h4>
    <p>
      Six models and 200 checkpoints (snapshots saved during training) are free on Hugging Face under the Open Aleph License. A license
      sets the legal terms of use, and this one covers only non-commercial, non-administrative uses such as research and teaching.
    </p>

    <!-- 2. Release kit -->
    <h3>Sample release kit</h3>
    <p><strong>Sample drafts</strong> I would prepare for the team’s review. They are not official Aleph Alpha communications.</p>

    <div class="card">
      <span class="label">Sample draft · LinkedIn post</span>
      <p>
        Can a language model work without a tokenizer and still compete? Our new report describes tokenizer-free HAT models with up to 70
        billion parameters, trained in English and German.
      </p>
      <p>Two findings:</p>
      <p>
        → Our 7B model, trained from scratch on one third of Llama 3.1’s data budget, is competitive with Llama 3.1 8B.<br />
        → On German text our models need fewer steps: 6.03 bytes per step on a German knowledge test, compared with 3.33 for Llama 3.1 8B.
      </p>
      <p>Models, 200 training checkpoints and our evaluation framework are public, so you can check the numbers yourself.</p>
      <p>Report: arxiv.org/abs/2603.15953<br />Models: huggingface.co/Aleph-Alpha and huggingface.co/Aleph-Alpha-Research</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <span class="label">Sample draft · Conference talk abstract</span>
      <h4>Training language models without a tokenizer: lessons from HAT models up to 70B parameters</h4>
      <p>
        Most language models depend on a tokenizer, a fixed vocabulary learned before training and usually tuned for English. We present
        the Hierarchical Autoregressive Transformer (HAT), in which small byte-level encoder and decoder modules surround a word-level
        backbone. We compare two routes: training a 7B model from scratch on nearly 4 trillion words, and converting pre-trained Llama 3.1
        8B and 70B models. We share English and German benchmark results, compression gains and what it took to serve hierarchical models
        in vLLM. We close with open questions and our 200 released checkpoints for studying how models learn.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <span class="label">Sample draft · 5-slide internal summary</span>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Why:</strong> fixed tokenizers split German and specialist words into many pieces.</li>
        <li><strong>How:</strong> HAT reads bytes, works in words and writes bytes, with no fixed vocabulary.</li>
        <li><strong>Results:</strong> German ARC Challenge 59.1% vs 47.4%; 6.03 vs 3.33 bytes per step on German text.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits:</strong> English and German only, no code or maths focus, 70B is experimental.</li>
        <li><strong>Release:</strong> six models, 200 checkpoints and eval-framework, with license terms.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

