    <h4>What other research labs do (examples only)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cohere Labs</strong> runs an <a href="https://cohere.com/research/open-science">Open Science Community</a> with talks by
        researchers and groups on topics such as multilingual AI and safety. Its <a href="https://cohere.com/research/scholars-program">Scholars Program</a>
        is a paid, remote-first position that pairs emerging researchers with mentors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>EleutherAI</strong>, a non-profit lab, <a href="https://www.eleuther.ai/about">works mainly in a public Discord server</a>
        shared by staff, volunteers and outside collaborators. The March 2026 report compares its 200 checkpoints to the 154 per model in
        EleutherAI’s Pythia suite.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ai2</strong> releases its <a href="https://allenai.org/olmo">Olmo models</a> with training data, code and checkpoints, and
        its Olmo page shows projects others built with them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Ideas I would test</h4>
    <p>Ideas to test with the team, not plans or promises.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>A checkpoint study circle.</strong> The report says the team has not yet analysed the 200 checkpoints. Inviting a few
        university groups to study them, with findings collected on one public page, would test whether outside researchers pick them up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A German evaluation sprint.</strong>
        <a href="https://github.com/Aleph-Alpha-Research/eval-framework">eval-framework</a> has over 90 tasks and
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Aleph-Alpha/MTBench-German">MTBench-German</a> is public. A one-day online sprint for
        student teams could show whether shared tools attract new contributors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An open spelling test.</strong> eval-framework supports perturbation testing, which changes inputs on purpose, for example
        with typos. A public notebook comparing HAT and tokenizer-based models on misspelled German text would let anyone repeat the test.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>“Built with GermanWeb”.</strong>
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Aleph-Alpha/Aleph-Alpha-GermanWeb">Aleph-Alpha-GermanWeb</a> had 898 downloads in 30
        days, all anonymous. An optional form on the dataset card asking what people built could turn some users into case studies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plain-language summaries.</strong> Technical blog posts could get a one-page summary in English and German, like the
        explainer above. Comparing reading time and shares with and without one would show whether it helps.
      </li>
    </ol>

